 "literal": "Plucking mountain pool flowers, weaving a garland, / the innocent girl with flower-like eyes who chases parrots from the field, / does she know or does she not? At midnight, / sighing like a sleeping elephant, my / heart is still with her.",
 "translation": "Plucking flowers from the mountain pool and weaving them into a garland, the innocent girl with flower-like eyes chases parrots from the millet field. Does she know of my state or not? At midnight, sighing deeply like a sleeping elephant, my heart still remains with her.",
 "modern": "சுனைப் பூக்களைப் பறித்து, மாலை தொடுத்து, தினைப்புனத்துக் கிளிகளை விரட்டும் மலர் போன்ற கண்களை உடைய அந்தப் பேதைப்பெண், என் நிலையை அறிந்துள்ளாளா அல்லது அறியவில்லையா? நள்ளிரவில், படுத்திருக்கும் யானையைப் போலப் பெருமூச்சு விட்டுக்கொண்டு, என் நெஞ்சம் மீண்டும் அவளிடமே தங்கியிருக்கிறது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the hero to himself after meeting the heroine in the mountain fields. He is back at his own place, but he is unable to sleep due to his intense love. He wonders if the innocent girl, who spends her days plucking pool flowers and chasing parrots, is aware of the effect she has had on him. The setting belongs to the Kuriñci tiṇai, which represents the union of lovers and the sweet anxiety of early love. The comparison of the hero's heavy midnight sighs to the deep breathing of a sleeping elephant beautifully conveys his profound longing and restlessness. Key words like 'pētai' (innocent girl) and 'paḷḷi yāṉai' (sleeping elephant) anchor the emotional and physical landscape of the poem.",
